#~
Virtual calls from several threads at once resolve to the right override.

A class caches the override each virtual declaration reaches for its
instances, keyed by (declaring class, method), and the first call through a
pair adds the entry. That cache was a plain hash map shared by every thread
with no lock (the code asked "thread safe?"; it was not): two threads making
first calls on the same class inserted at the same time, and an insert that
grows the map frees the buckets a lookup on another thread is reading. The
result was a wrong method, a Nil dereference, or a crash, but only when two
first calls met, so almost never.

This is the widest that window gets: 40 classes with 8 virtual methods each,
none of them called before 8 threads are released together to call all of
them. Every thread must compute the same total as the arithmetic says.
~#
use System.Concurrency;

class Shape {
	New() {}

	method : virtual : public : F0(x : Int) ~ Int;
	method : virtual : public : F1(x : Int) ~ Int;
	method : virtual : public : F2(x : Int) ~ Int;
	method : virtual : public : F3(x : Int) ~ Int;
	method : virtual : public : F4(x : Int) ~ Int;
	method : virtual : public : F5(x : Int) ~ Int;
	method : virtual : public : F6(x : Int) ~ Int;
	method : virtual : public : F7(x : Int) ~ Int;
}

class S0 from Shape {
	New() { Parent(); }
	method : public : F0(x : Int) ~ Int { return x + 0; }
	method : public : F1(x : Int) ~ Int { return x + 1; }
	method : public : F2(x : Int) ~ Int { return x + 2; }
	method : public : F3(x : Int) ~ Int { return x + 3; }
	method : public : F4(x : Int) ~ Int { return x + 4; }
	method : public : F5(x : Int) ~ Int { return x + 5; }
	method : public : F6(x : Int) ~ Int { return x + 6; }
	method : public : F7(x : Int) ~ Int { return x + 7; }
}

